# m3_full_data.py - Complete Module 3 topics data
M3_DATA = {'m3-t1': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why is feeding raw daily temperature (°C) into a crop yield model far less predictive than transforming it into Growing Degree Days (GDD)?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Plants do not experience calendar days; they accumulate physiological thermal heat! A crop does not grow below its base temperature (e.g., 10°C for maize), and growth halts above heat stress thresholds (30°C). Growing Degree Days `GDD = max(0, (T_max + T_min)/2 - T_base)` converts calendar time into biological physiological time, directly tracking plant phenological stages.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> Why does creating rolling lagged rainfall features (e.g., rainfall over the previous 14 days) matter more for predicting harvest yield than the rainfall recorded on harvest day?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Crops have critical phenological windows (flowering, silking, grain filling). A 50mm rainfall event during flowering determines pollination success, while the same rainfall on harvest day merely delays machinery. Lagged rolling features capture moisture availability during sensitive historical growth phases that directly dictate final yield.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live GDD & CWSI Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live agro-meteorological feature engineering demo, what physiological base temperature was subtracted when computing daily Growing Degree Days (GDD) for maize?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) T_base = 10.0°C, the minimum physiological threshold below which maize vegetative growth halts</li>\n                        <li>B) 0.0°C (freezing point of water)</li>\n                        <li>C) 100.0°C</li>\n                        <li>D) -10.0°C</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>T_base = 10.0°C, the biological minimum threshold below which maize stops development.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> GDD accumulates thermal time above a species-specific baseline. For maize, biochemical enzymatic processes cease below 10°C, making T_base = 10°C the standard agronomic reference.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> 0°C is the baseline for winter wheat and cold-hardy cereals, not warm-season maize.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> 100°C is boiling point of water and lethal to all plant life.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Negative temperatures reflect freezing conditions with zero biological growth.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Agro-Meteorological Feature Studio):</strong> During the Feature Architecture Studio, which engineered feature proved most effective at capturing acute mid-season crop water stress?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Crop Water Stress Index (CWSI) computed from canopy-to-air temperature differences and Vapor Pressure Deficit (VPD)</li>\n                        <li>B) The farmer\'s email address length</li>\n                        <li>C) The absolute latitude integer</li>\n                        <li>D) Total historical rainfall over the last 100 years</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Crop Water Stress Index (CWSI) linking canopy temperature depression with atmospheric VPD.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> CWSI leverages thermal physics: well-watered plants transpire rapidly, evaporatively cooling leaves 2–5°C below ambient air; water-stressed plants close stomata, causing leaf temperatures to spike above air temperature.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Text string lengths have zero biological or physical relationship to crop water status.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Raw integer latitudes ignore localized weather and soil dynamics.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Century-long historical rainfall fails to capture acute weekly soil moisture deficits.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building an Agricultural Feature Pipeline, which Pandas expression generated a 14-day cumulative rainfall rolling feature?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) df[\'rain_14d_sum\'] = df[\'daily_rainfall\'].rolling(window=14, min_periods=1).sum()</li>\n                        <li>B) df[\'rain_14d_sum\'] = df[\'daily_rainfall\'] * 14</li>\n                        <li>C) df[\'daily_rainfall\'].cumsum().tail(14)</li>\n                        <li>D) df[\'daily_rainfall\'].shift(-14)</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>df[\'daily_rainfall\'].rolling(window=14, min_periods=1).sum() computed the backward-looking 14-day rolling sum.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Rolling window summation aggregates the past 14 days of rainfall for each row, with `min_periods=1` ensuring valid accumulations even at the start of the time series.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Multiplying by 14 assumes identical rainfall every day.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> `.cumsum().tail(14)` extracts only the final 14 days of total cumulative annual rainfall.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> `.shift(-14)` looks 14 days into the future, creating temporal look-ahead leakage.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm3-t2': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why do Tree Ensemble models (Random Forest and XGBoost) consistently outperform Linear Regression in predicting regional crop yields?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Agricultural yield responses are inherently non-linear and governed by complex biological interactions (e.g., high nitrogen fertilizer increases yield *only if* soil moisture is adequate, but triggers toxic salt stress if soil is dry). Linear models cannot capture these multi-way feature interactions without manual polynomial expansion, whereas Decision Trees partition feature spaces into non-linear interaction boxes automatically.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> What is the danger of relying solely on satellite NDVI to predict final harvest yield three months before harvest?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> NDVI measures green canopy biomass, not reproductive grain fill! A field can have a lush, dark-green vegetative canopy (high NDVI), but if a severe heatwave or water deficit hits during pollination/flowering, flowers abort and grain ears remain empty (\'chaff\'). Early NDVI must be combined with post-flowering meteorological thermal indices for accurate yield prediction.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Ensemble Comparison Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration comparing Random Forest and Gradient Boosting on district-level soybean yield, why did Gradient Boosting achieve lower RMSE?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Gradient Boosting builds trees sequentially to iteratively correct the residual errors of preceding trees, capturing subtle non-linear climate thresholds</li>\n                        <li>B) Gradient Boosting runs without using any computer memory</li>\n                        <li>C) Random Forest only works on image data</li>\n                        <li>D) Gradient Boosting ignores weather data</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Gradient Boosting sequentially minimizes residual errors, fitting complex non-linear yield responses.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> While Random Forest trains independent trees in parallel and averages them, Gradient Boosting fits successive trees to the pseudo-residuals of the loss function, steadily reducing bias on complex biological interactions.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Gradient boosting utilizes standard RAM and CPU/GPU memory.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Random Forest is a general tabular estimator widely used across regression and classification.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Weather features are essential inputs in crop yield gradient boosting.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Crop Model Benchmarking Studio):</strong> During the Model Benchmarking Studio, how did teams handle extreme drought years (e.g., 2016 severe monsoon failure) where crop yields plummeted by 70%?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) By verifying that the training set included historical drought years and applying sample weighting to prevent the ensemble from under-predicting extreme climate tail events</li>\n                        <li>B) By deleting the 2016 data as an \'unrealistic outlier\'</li>\n                        <li>C) By assuming drought never happens</li>\n                        <li>D) By setting all predicted yields to the mean</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Retained extreme drought years and applied sample weighting to capture climatic tail risk.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Deleting climate shock years blinds predictive models to future drought events. Retaining extreme events with proper loss weighting ensures models learn catastrophic crop failure triggers.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Deleting extreme drought records guarantees models fail during the next climate anomaly.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Ignoring drought risk produces catastrophic farm loan underwriting defaults.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Mean prediction destroys all variance and predictive capability.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building a Crop Yield Prediction Ensemble, what Scikit-Learn class blended predictions from a Random Forest and an XGBoost model into an optimal weighted meta-model?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) sklearn.ensemble.VotingRegressor or StackingRegressor</li>\n                        <li>B) sklearn.neighbors.KNeighborsClassifier</li>\n                        <li>C) sklearn.cluster.KMeans</li>\n                        <li>D) sklearn.naive_bayes.GaussianNB</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>VotingRegressor or StackingRegressor combined diverse individual model predictions.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> `VotingRegressor` averages individual model predictions, while `StackingRegressor` trains a final meta-regressor on cross-validated out-of-fold predictions, leveraging complementary model strengths.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> KNeighborsClassifier is a classification algorithm, not a continuous yield meta-regressor.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> KMeans is unsupervised clustering.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> GaussianNB is a probabilistic classifier for categorical labels.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm3-t3': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why does Ridge Regression (L2) keep all agricultural features with small shrunk weights, while Lasso Regression (L1) forces redundant features to exactly zero?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Geometry of the penalty functions! Lasso\'s L1 penalty (`sum(|w|)`) has sharp diamond corners at the coordinate axes, causing gradient paths to hit zero and performing automated feature selection. Ridge\'s L2 penalty (`sum(w²)`) is a smooth sphere, shrinking coefficients asymptotically toward zero but retaining all features. ElasticNet blends both L1 and L2 penalties.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> When predicting wheat yield across 100 variables (soil, weather, vegetation indices), why is ElasticNet often superior to pure Lasso?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Collinear features! In agriculture, many features are strongly correlated (e.g., Sentinel-2 Band 8, Band 8A, and NDVI). When faced with a group of correlated features, pure Lasso arbitrarily picks one and zeroes out the rest. ElasticNet groups correlated features together, shrinking their weights cooperatively while preserving structural predictive signals.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Non-Linear Yield Curve Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration fitting crop yield against nitrogen application, why did polynomial regression with ElasticNet regularization outperform standard linear regression?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) It captured the biological Mitscherlich diminishing returns curve (quadratic peak followed by toxic yield decline) while regularizing redundant polynomial powers</li>\n                        <li>B) It assumed infinite linear yield growth</li>\n                        <li>C) It converted crop yield into a categorical string</li>\n                        <li>D) It deleted all fertilizer data points</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Captured non-linear biological diminishing returns while regularizing higher-order polynomial terms.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Crop nutrient uptake follows non-linear polynomial response curves. Regularized polynomial regression captures the parabolic inflection point where additional fertilizer becomes toxic while preventing overfitting.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Assuming infinite linear growth violates biological limits and kills crops through over-fertilization.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Regression predicts continuous numerical yields, not categorical strings.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Deleting fertilizer data removes the primary explanatory variable.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Multicollinearity Regularization Studio):</strong> During the Multi-Collinear Sensor Studio, why did student teams choose ElasticNet over Lasso when regularizing 25 correlated multi-spectral vegetation indices?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Lasso arbitrarily selected one random spectral index and discarded the other 24, while ElasticNet retained groups of correlated indices with balanced weights</li>\n                        <li>B) ElasticNet runs in zero CPU clock cycles</li>\n                        <li>C) Lasso is not supported in Scikit-Learn</li>\n                        <li>D) ElasticNet only works on integer inputs</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>ElasticNet stabilized correlated feature groups, avoiding Lasso\'s erratic single-feature selection.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> When features are highly correlated (as in adjacent multi-spectral bands), Lasso exhibits grouping instability by picking one feature at random. ElasticNet\'s L2 component encourages grouped selection, retaining collective signal.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> ElasticNet uses standard numerical coordinate descent optimization.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Lasso is fully supported in Scikit-Learn via `sklearn.linear_model.Lasso`.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> ElasticNet natively processes continuous floating-point features.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task training an ElasticNet Yield Forecaster, what hyperparameter balanced the ratio between L1 (Lasso) and L2 (Ridge) penalties?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) l1_ratio (where 1.0 is pure Lasso and 0.0 is pure Ridge)</li>\n                        <li>B) max_depth</li>\n                        <li>C) n_estimators</li>\n                        <li>D) criterion=\'gini\'</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>l1_ratio controlled the convex combination between L1 and L2 regularization penalties.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> In Scikit-Learn\'s `ElasticNet(alpha=..., l1_ratio=...)`, `l1_ratio` specifies the mixing balance: `l1_ratio=1` corresponds to pure Lasso, while `l1_ratio=0` corresponds to pure Ridge.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> max_depth is a decision tree hyperparameter.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> n_estimators specifies tree count in ensemble models.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> criterion=\'gini\' is an impurity split metric for classification trees.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm3-t4': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> A weather station is located 15 kilometers away from an experimental farm. Why can trusting its rainfall data lead to completely incorrect crop yield models?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Tropical and convective monsoon rainfall is hyper-localized: a thunderstorm can dump 60mm of rain on the weather station while leaving the farm 15 km away completely dry, or vice versa! Agronomic models must ingest gridded reanalysis data (ERA5, IMD Gridded) or integrate on-farm IoT rain gauges to eliminate spatial weather mismatch.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> What is \'Heat Stress Degree-Hours\' (HSDH), and why is it more critical for wheat yield than average daily temperature?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Wheat pollination is damaged by short bursts of acute heat: temperatures exceeding 32°C for even 3 hours during anthesis cause pollen sterility, devastating harvest yield. A daily average of 24°C conceals this lethal 3-hour spike. Heat Stress Degree-Hours accumulates the exact duration and intensity of temperatures exceeding critical physiological thresholds.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live API Weather Integration Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration connecting to open-meteo APIs, how were hourly weather observations temporally aligned with daily farm harvest ledgers?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Resampling hourly streams to daily aggregates (computing daily T_max, T_min, total precipitation, and hours > 35°C) and joining on date index</li>\n                        <li>B) Deleting all weather records except the first hour of each month</li>\n                        <li>C) Shuffling weather timestamps randomly</li>\n                        <li>D) Converting dates into latitude coordinates</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Resampled hourly weather to daily agronomic summary metrics and joined on calendar dates.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Raw weather APIs stream high-frequency hourly records. Agronomic integration requires aggregating hours into biologically meaningful daily metrics (diurnal temperature range, cumulative rainfall, extreme heat hours) before joining with plot records.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Deleting 99% of weather observations discards storm events and diurnal extremes.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Randomizing timestamps destroys all temporal alignment with crop growth.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Dates cannot be converted into geographic coordinates.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Micro-Climate Interpolation Studio):</strong> During the Weather Interpolation Studio, how did teams estimate rainfall for an isolated farm situated between three distant regional IMD weather stations?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Inverse Distance Weighting (IDW) interpolation: weighting rainfall contributions inversely proportional to station distance squared</li>\n                        <li>B) Using the rainfall of the station that logged the highest number</li>\n                        <li>C) Averaging the three stations with equal 33% weights regardless of distance</li>\n                        <li>D) Guessing zero rainfall</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Inverse Distance Weighting (IDW) assigned greater weight to geographically closer stations.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Spatial interpolation via IDW assigns higher mathematical weights to nearby stations based on distance decay, generating realistic spatial estimates across unmonitored farm topography.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Picking the maximum station overestimates rainfall and conceals dry zones.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Equal weighting ignores distance, giving a station 100 km away the same influence as one 2 km away.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Assuming zero rain introduces extreme drought bias.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task calculating Heat Stress Lags, which Pandas code computed the number of consecutive days where daily T_max exceeded 35.0°C?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) (df[\'t_max\'] > 35.0).astype(int).groupby((~(df[\'t_max\'] > 35.0)).cumsum()).cumsum()</li>\n                        <li>B) df[\'t_max\'].sum()</li>\n                        <li>C) df[\'t_max\'] == 35.0</li>\n                        <li>D) df[\'t_max\'].diff()</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Grouped consecutive boolean exceedance masks to calculate cumulative consecutive heatwave duration.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Tracking heatwave persistence requires identifying contiguous blocks of threshold exceedance, accomplished in Pandas by grouping boolean masks against cumulative inversion boundaries.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> `.sum()` aggregates all temperatures across the year into an uninterpretable scalar.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Equality (`== 35.0`) checks for exact float matches, missing days with 36°C or 40°C.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> `.diff()` computes day-over-day changes, not heatwave run lengths.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm3-t5': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why is collecting 10 physical soil core samples per acre across a 500-acre farm economically impossible, and how does spatial interpolation (Kriging) solve this?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Laboratory soil testing costs Rs. 500–1,500 per sample. Taking 5,000 samples would cost Rs. 25–75 lakhs, bankrupting the farm project. Instead, agronomists take 50 scientifically distributed samples (using stratified grid or management zone sampling) and apply geostatistical Ordinary Kriging to generate high-resolution continuous fertility surfaces (pH, N, P, K) across the remaining 99% of unmeasured acreage.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> What is a \'Semivariogram\' in soil geostatistics, and what does the \'Range\' parameter tell an agronomist about soil spatial variability?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> A semivariogram plots variance between pairs of soil samples as a function of the distance separating them. As distance increases, variance rises until it hits a plateau (the Sill). The distance at which it plateaus is the \'Range\': beyond this distance, soil samples are spatially independent. The Range dictates the maximum allowable spacing between soil probes.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Kriging Spatial Interpolation Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration of spatial soil interpolation, why is Ordinary Kriging preferred over simple nearest-neighbor interpolation when mapping soil pH?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Kriging models spatial autocorrelation via a semivariogram, providing both optimal Best Linear Unbiased Estimates (BLUE) and an explicit spatial uncertainty/variance map</li>\n                        <li>B) Nearest-neighbor runs only on deep learning GPUs</li>\n                        <li>C) Kriging changes the physical pH of the soil</li>\n                        <li>D) Kriging requires no input coordinates</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Kriging models spatial autocorrelation to provide optimal estimates and an explicit prediction variance map.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Unlike deterministic interpolation methods, Kriging is a geostatistical estimator that models the spatial covariance structure of the landscape, outputting predicted nutrient surfaces alongside statistical error variance rasters.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Nearest-neighbor is a simple distance algorithm running on basic CPUs.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Software algorithms model data; they do not alter physical soil chemistry.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Kriging is fundamentally geospatial and requires exact (x, y) coordinates.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Soil Fertility Sampling Density Studio):</strong> During the Sampling Density Studio, what parameter of the experimental semivariogram determined the maximum distance between soil core samples to ensure spatial dependence?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) The Range &mdash; the spatial separation distance beyond which sample points cease to be spatially correlated</li>\n                        <li>B) The Nugget alone</li>\n                        <li>C) The color of the soil sampling bags</li>\n                        <li>D) The time of day the sample was tested</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>The Range parameter defined the spatial correlation boundary for sampling grid design.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> In geostatistics, the semivariogram Range represents the distance threshold of spatial autocorrelation. To capture spatial continuity, sample spacing must be closer than the Range.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> The Nugget represents measurement error and micro-scale variability at zero distance.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Bag color has zero bearing on spatial geostatistics.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Testing time does not dictate geographic spatial correlation distance.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task interpolating soil pH across an experimental field, which SciPy module performed 2D Radial Basis Function (RBF) spatial interpolation?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) scipy.interpolate.Rbf(x_coords, y_coords, ph_values, function=\'multiquadric\')</li>\n                        <li>B) scipy.fft.fft2()</li>\n                        <li>C) scipy.optimize.minimize()</li>\n                        <li>D) scipy.signal.convolve2d()</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>scipy.interpolate.Rbf fitted smooth spatial multiquadric surfaces across scattered coordinates.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> SciPy\'s `Rbf` class performs mesh-free spatial interpolation on scattered 2D points, fitting smooth continuous surfaces (multiquadric, thin-plate spline) across field coordinates.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> fft2() computes 2D Fast Fourier Transforms for frequency domain signal processing.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> optimize.minimize() is for numerical parameter optimization.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> convolve2d() performs spatial image filtering, not scattered point interpolation.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm3-t6': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why does healthy green vegetation reflect near-infrared (NIR) light intensely while absorbing red light, and why is this optical contrast the foundation of all satellite crop health monitoring?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Plant physiology! Chlorophyll pigments in leaf chloroplasts absorb red light (0.66 µm) to fuel photosynthesis. Simultaneously, the spongy mesophyll cell structure inside healthy leaves reflects near-infrared radiation (0.85 µm) to prevent leaf solar overheating. When a plant experiences drought or pathogen attack, mesophyll cells collapse and chlorophyll degrades, causing red reflection to rise and NIR to collapse—a dramatic optical shift measured by NDVI.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> Why does NDVI saturate (flatten out) in dense canopies (e.g., peak growth sugarcane or maize), and what advanced spectral index overcomes this saturation?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Once a crop canopy achieves full closure (Leaf Area Index > 3), red light absorption maxes out at nearly 100%, causing the red reflectance term to stop changing even as biomass increases. Advanced indices like the Normalized Difference Red Edge (NDRE) use the \'RedEdge\' transition band (0.705–0.740 µm), which penetrates deeper into dense canopies without saturating.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Multispectral Canopy Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live multispectral demonstration, what formula computed the Normalized Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI) from calibrated Sentinel-2 surface reflectance bands?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) NDVI = (B8_NIR - B4_Red) / (B8_NIR + B4_Red)</li>\n                        <li>B) NDVI = (B4_Red - B8_NIR) / (B4_Red + B8_NIR)</li>\n                        <li>C) NDVI = B8_NIR * B4_Red</li>\n                        <li>D) NDVI = B8_NIR / 255.0</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>NDVI = (NIR - Red) / (NIR + Red) normalized contrast between cellular reflectance and chlorophyll absorption.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> NDVI computes the normalized ratio of the difference between near-infrared (Sentinel-2 Band 8) and red (Band 4) over their sum, producing values between -1.0 (water/barren) and +1.0 (dense healthy canopy).</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Inverting numerator terms produces negative indices for healthy vegetation.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Multiplying bands scales with solar illumination rather than measuring normalized biological contrast.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Dividing NIR by 255 does not contrast with chlorophyll absorption.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Canopy Saturation & NDRE Studio):</strong> During the Canopy Saturation Studio, why did student teams switch from NDVI to NDRE (Normalized Difference Red Edge) when scouting late-stage commercial sugarcane?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Because standard NDVI saturated near 0.85 in dense canopy, while the RedEdge band penetrated deep foliage layers to detect localized sub-canopy nitrogen deficiencies</li>\n                        <li>B) Because sugarcane cannot be detected by NIR sensors</li>\n                        <li>C) Because NDRE makes the satellite orbit faster</li>\n                        <li>D) Because red light travels faster through cane stalks</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>NDRE penetrated dense foliage, avoiding NDVI canopy saturation and revealing sub-canopy nitrogen stress.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> In dense, high-biomass crops (sugarcane, maize), red absorption reaches maximum extinction, saturating NDVI. The Red Edge band (705–740nm) has lower absorption, penetrating deeper into multiple canopy layers.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Sugarcane mesophyll cells reflect NIR intensely.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Spectral indices are mathematical calculations and have zero impact on satellite orbital mechanics.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Light speeds in air are constant regardless of crop species.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task calculating Crop Health Indices, how did you handle potential zero-division runtime errors over water or barren soil pixels?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) np.where((nir + red) == 0, 0, (nir - red) / (nir + red + 1e-8))</li>\n                        <li>B) Letting Python crash with a ZeroDivisionError</li>\n                        <li>C) Setting all pixels to 1.0</li>\n                        <li>D) Deleting the image array</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Added an epsilon denominator term (1e-8) or used np.where() to safely avoid zero division.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Defensive raster algebra adds an infinitesimal epsilon constant (e.g., 1e-8) or uses `np.where()` to prevent fatal division-by-zero exceptions on completely dark or masked zero-reflectance pixels.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Allowing unhandled crashes halts batch satellite processing pipelines.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Setting to 1.0 falsely classifies water bodies as ultra-dense healthy vegetation.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Deleting arrays discards entire satellite scenes.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm3-t7': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> A farmer observes yellowing leaves in a tomato field and assumes it is nitrogen deficiency. How can an automated diagnostic system differentiate nutrient deficiency from Early Blight fungal infection?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Symptom morphology! Nitrogen deficiency causes generalized chlorosis starting on older leaves with uniform pale yellowing along leaf margins. Early Blight (Alternaria solani) produces discrete, irregular brown necrotic lesions characterized by concentric rings (\'target-board\' or \'bullseye\' pattern) surrounded by a yellow chlorotic halo. Computer vision models recognize these distinct spatial geometries and textures.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> Why is diagnosing crop diseases from RGB smartphone photographs taken under direct midday sun prone to severe misclassification?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Harsh direct sunlight produces intense specular reflections (glare) and deep, hard-edged shadow occlusions. Glare washes out subtle necrotic fungal discoloration, while shadows mimic dark fungal spots. AI vision pipelines must apply color normalization, shadow removal, or instruct users to capture foliage under diffuse lighting.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Visual Pathology Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live pathology demonstration, what distinct visual geometric pattern distinguished Alternaria fungal lesions from bacterial leaf spot?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Concentric circular rings forming a \'target-board\' pattern with a surrounding yellow chlorotic halo</li>\n                        <li>B) Square pixels arranged in a checkerboard pattern</li>\n                        <li>C) Perfectly uniform leaf discoloration across all veins</li>\n                        <li>D) Pure white metallic reflections</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Concentric circular rings forming a \'target-board\' pattern characteristic of Alternaria fungal lesions.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Alternaria solani creates characteristic concentric dark brown rings surrounded by yellow chlorosis due to cyclical mycelial expansion and toxin release.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Biological pathogens grow in organic patterns, not artificial digital square checkerboards.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Uniform chlorosis typically indicates systemic nutrient deficiencies, not localized fungal lesions.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Leaves do not emit metallic reflections.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Diagnostic Decision Rules Studio):</strong> During the Pathology Studio, what environmental co-factor was integrated into the diagnostic decision engine to distinguish fungal blight from physiological drought scorching?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Relative humidity history (>85% relative humidity for 48 hours strongly favors fungal spore germination over simple heat scorch)</li>\n                        <li>B) The farmer\'s footwear type</li>\n                        <li>C) The price of diesel fuel</li>\n                        <li>D) The day of the week</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Relative humidity history (>85% for 48 hours) confirming physiological conditions for fungal sporulation.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Fungal spores require free moisture and high humidity (>85–90%) to germinate and penetrate leaf stomata, providing a crucial environmental validation signal.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Footwear has zero physiological connection to plant pathology.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Diesel prices do not impact microscopic fungal spores.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Pathogens respond to biology and physics, not calendar days.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building a Rule-Based Crop Disease Classifier, what image feature extracted the ratio of diseased lesion pixels to total healthy green leaf area?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Color thresholding in HSV color space to segment brown lesion masks vs green canopy masks</li>\n                        <li>B) Counting the number of letters in the filename</li>\n                        <li>C) Summing all raw pixel values without color conversion</li>\n                        <li>D) Converting the image to a sound file</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Segmented lesion masks versus green canopy masks using HSV color thresholding.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> HSV color space cleanly separates chromaticity (Hue) from lighting intensity (Value), allowing precise color mask thresholding to isolate brown diseased spots from healthy green tissue.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Filenames carry zero visual pixel information.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Summing raw RGB values without thresholding yields a meaningless brightness total.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Audio conversion is completely irrelevant to optical leaf pathology.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm3-t8': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why is the HSV (Hue, Saturation, Value) color space far more robust than standard RGB for isolating diseased plant foliage under changing outdoor sunlight?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> In RGB, a cloud passing overhead changes all three channels (R, G, and B) simultaneously because brightness is entangled with color. In HSV, lighting intensity is isolated completely into the \'V\' (Value) channel. The \'H\' (Hue) channel stays constant, representing true color pigment (green foliage = Hue 35–85) regardless of whether the leaf is in direct sun or shadow.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> What role do morphological operations (Erosion and Dilation) play in cleaning noisy agricultural computer vision segmentations?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Outdoor farm images contain dust particles, camera sensor salt-and-pepper noise, and tiny dew reflections. Morphological \'Opening\' (erosion followed by dilation) removes isolated noisy white speckles without altering the boundary of actual disease lesions. Morphological \'Closing\' bridges tiny cracks and fills dark holes inside solid leaf contours.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live OpenCV HSV Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live OpenCV demonstration, how was healthy green canopy segmented away from dark agricultural background soil?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) By converting from BGR to HSV and thresholding Hue between 35 and 85 with cv2.inRange()</li>\n                        <li>B) By deleting all green pixels from the image</li>\n                        <li>C) By blurring the image until it became gray</li>\n                        <li>D) By setting camera exposure to zero</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Converted to HSV and applied cv2.inRange() with Hue boundaries (35 to 85) to isolate green chlorophyll.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> In OpenCV HSV (where Hue ranges from 0 to 180), green vegetation sits tightly between Hue 35 and 85, allowing robust binary thresholding of plant canopies independent of shadows.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Deleting green pixels removes the very crop canopy you intend to segment.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Excessive blurring destroys all edge and contour resolution.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Zero exposure produces a completely black image.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Morphological Filtering Studio):</strong> During the Morphological Filtering Studio, what OpenCV sequence removed tiny dust speckles and filled small holes in leaf contour masks?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) cv2.morphologyEx() with cv2.MORPH_OPEN (remove noise) followed by cv2.MORPH_CLOSE (fill small holes)</li>\n                        <li>B) cv2.invert() twice</li>\n                        <li>C) Resizing the image to 1x1 pixel</li>\n                        <li>D) Adding Gaussian noise to the image</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>cv2.morphologyEx() applying MORPH_OPEN to strip noise and MORPH_CLOSE to close internal voids.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Morphological Opening (erode then dilate) removes tiny foreground speckles; Closing (dilate then erode) fills small holes within segmented contours, creating clean binary masks.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Inverting twice returns the original noisy image unchanged.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Shrinking to 1x1 collapses the image to an uninformative single pixel.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Adding noise increases image degradation rather than cleaning it.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task detecting leaf disease lesions, which OpenCV function extracted geometric contours and computed lesion surface area in pixels?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) contours, _ = cv2.findContours(mask, cv2.RETR_EXTERNAL, cv2.CHAIN_APPROX_SIMPLE) followed by cv2.contourArea(cnt)</li>\n                        <li>B) cv2.imwrite(\'leaf.jpg\', mask)</li>\n                        <li>C) cv2.waitKey(0)</li>\n                        <li>D) cv2.destroyAllWindows()</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>cv2.findContours() extracted boundary coordinates and cv2.contourArea() calculated pixel areas.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> `cv2.findContours()` traces spatial topological boundaries of segmented binary masks, and `cv2.contourArea()` integrates pixel coordinates to compute exact lesion surface area.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> imwrite writes an image file to disk without calculating contours.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> waitKey pauses execution for keyboard input.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> destroyAllWindows closes graphical UI windows.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm3-t9': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why do Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) succeed in detecting microscopic leaf spots where traditional Multi-Layer Perceptrons (MLPs) fail completely?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Spatial locality and parameter sharing! An MLP flattens a 512x512 image into 262,144 independent inputs, losing all 2D spatial relationships and requiring billions of weights. A CNN slides small shared convolutional kernels (e.g., 3x3) across the image, preserving spatial neighborhood context and detecting edges, textures, and lesions regardless of where they appear on the leaf (translation invariance).</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> How do hierarchical layers in a deep CNN mirror how an agronomist visually diagnoses a sick plant?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Hierarchical feature abstraction! Early convolutional layers detect simple low-level primitives: sharp edges, color transitions, and texture gradients. Middle layers combine edges into geometric shapes (vein structures, circular lesion halos). Deep layers assemble these shapes into high-level semantic pathology concepts: \'powdery mildew mycelial patch\' or \'downy mildew angular lesion\'.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live CNN Feature Map Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration visualizing intermediate CNN feature activations on a diseased maize leaf, what visual features were captured by the earliest convolutional layers?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Low-level edge boundaries, high-frequency textural contrasts, and color gradients along leaf veins</li>\n                        <li>B) Fully classified disease diagnoses with confidence scores</li>\n                        <li>C) The exact date of planting</li>\n                        <li>D) An audio file of insect chewing</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Low-level edge boundaries, vein textures, and localized color gradients.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> First-layer convolutional filters act as Gabor-like edge and color detectors, preserving high-resolution spatial details before pooling layers downsample the representation.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Semantic classification happens at the final fully-connected softmax layer, not early conv layers.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Conv layers process visual pixels, not external planting metadata.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Vision CNNs operate on optical images, not acoustic signals.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Convolutional Kernel Architecture Studio):</strong> During the CNN Architecture Studio, why did student teams insert MaxPooling layers (e.g., 2x2 pool with stride 2) after convolutional blocks?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) To downsample spatial dimensions by 50%, reducing parameter computation while conferring translation invariance</li>\n                        <li>B) To increase image resolution by 400%</li>\n                        <li>C) To convert 2D images into 3D holograms</li>\n                        <li>D) To force all weights to zero</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Downsampled spatial dimensions to reduce computation and build spatial translation invariance.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> MaxPooling extracts dominant features within pooling windows, halving spatial dimensions, expanding the receptive field of downstream layers, and making representations robust to small spatial shifts.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> MaxPooling downsamples dimensions; it does not upscale resolution.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Pooling produces 2D feature maps, not 3D holograms.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Zeroing weights destroys neural network representations.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building a Miniature Agro-CNN in PyTorch or Keras, which layer flattened the 2D feature maps before passing them into the final dense classification layer?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) layers.Flatten() or torch.flatten(x, 1)</li>\n                        <li>B) layers.Dropout(0.5)</li>\n                        <li>C) layers.BatchNormalization()</li>\n                        <li>D) layers.Activation(\'relu\')</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Flatten() reshaped 3D feature tensors (channels x height x width) into a 1D vector for dense classification.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Dense (fully-connected) layers expect 1D vector inputs; `Flatten()` bridges convolutional tensor feature maps to the output classification logits.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Dropout randomly zeroes out activations during training to curb overfitting.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> BatchNormalization standardizes activations across batches.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> ReLU applies element-wise non-linear activation.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm3-t10': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why should an AgriTech startup never train a 50-layer deep neural network for crop disease classification completely from scratch on their own dataset of 1,000 farm photos?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Training from scratch with 1,000 photos guarantees severe overfitting and poor convergence! Deep networks (like ResNet or MobileNet) have tens of millions of parameters and require millions of images to learn general edge and texture filters. Transfer Learning allows you to take a network pre-trained on ImageNet (1.4 million images) and simply fine-tune the final classification head on your 1,000 crop images in minutes with state-of-the-art accuracy.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> Why is MobileNetV2 preferred over giant models like Vision Transformers (ViT) or ResNet-152 when deploying crop disease models on cheap Android smartphones used by farmers?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Compute and memory constraints! ResNet-152 requires gigabytes of RAM, hundreds of megabytes of storage, and substantial GPU power. MobileNetV2 uses depthwise separable convolutions and inverted residuals, achieving high accuracy with a tiny 14 MB footprint, running inference in 30 milliseconds directly on low-cost smartphone CPUs without needing cloud connectivity.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Transfer Learning Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live transfer learning demonstration, which layer modification adapted a pre-trained ImageNet MobileNetV2 model (originally 1,000 classes) to detect 5 regional coffee leaf diseases?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Replacing the final dense classification head with a new Dense layer with 5 output units and a Softmax activation, while freezing the convolutional base feature extractor</li>\n                        <li>B) Retraining all 50 million weights from random initialization</li>\n                        <li>C) Deleting all convolutional layers and keeping only the input layer</li>\n                        <li>D) Multiplying all weights by 5</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Replaced the top classification layer with a 5-unit Softmax head while freezing base feature weights.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Transfer learning freezes the rich, general-purpose feature representations in the pre-trained convolutional backbone, training only the newly attached final classification layer on specific crop disease classes.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Retraining all weights from scratch on a small agricultural dataset triggers severe overfitting.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Deleting convolutional layers destroys all visual feature extraction capabilities.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Multiplying weights by 5 distorts learned filter representations.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Mobile Farm Edge AI Studio):</strong> During the Edge AI Studio, what post-training optimization technique compressed the PyTorch/TensorFlow crop disease model from 60 MB down to 15 MB for instant mobile app loading?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Post-Training INT8 Quantization: converting 32-bit floating-point weights (FP32) to 8-bit integers (INT8) with negligible loss in validation accuracy</li>\n                        <li>B) Deleting 75% of the code comments</li>\n                        <li>C) Zipping the file with a password</li>\n                        <li>D) Converting the model into an Excel spreadsheet</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>INT8 Quantization reduced model size by 75% and boosted mobile CPU inference speed with minimal accuracy drop.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Quantization compresses FP32 weights into 8-bit integers (INT8), slashing memory footprints by 4x and enabling hardware acceleration on mobile ARM NEON/NPU chipsets.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Compiled binary weights do not contain code comments.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Zipping files requires unzipping into RAM at runtime, offering no memory optimization.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Neural network tensors cannot be executed inside Excel spreadsheets.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task fine-tuning MobileNetV2 on crop foliage, what learning rate strategy prevented destroying pre-trained weights during the second-stage fine-tuning phase?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Unfreezing the top convolutional blocks and training with a very small learning rate (e.g., 1e-5), an order of magnitude smaller than initial head training</li>\n                        <li>B) Increasing the learning rate to 1.0</li>\n                        <li>C) Disabling the optimizer completely</li>\n                        <li>D) Setting epochs to zero</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Used a very small learning rate (1e-5) during fine-tuning to protect pre-trained feature weights.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Aggressive learning rates destroy pre-trained feature representations (\'catastrophic forgetting\'). Fine-tuning unfreezes upper layers using a delicate, small learning rate to adapt filters to subtle plant pathology details.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> A large learning rate (1.0) completely destabilizes gradient descent and wipes out pre-trained weights.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Disabling optimizers stops all parameter updates.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Zero epochs skips training completely.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm3-t11': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> The PlantVillage dataset contains 54,000+ laboratory photos of diseased leaves against plain gray or black backgrounds. Why does a model trained strictly on PlantVillage often achieve 99% laboratory accuracy but drop to 40% when tested on a real farm?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Domain Shift and Background Bias! In PlantVillage, leaves are detached, flattened on white/gray paper, and illuminated by studio lamps. In real fields, leaves are attached to living bushes with complex backgrounds (soil, weeds, neighboring leaves), moving in wind, and illuminated by harsh sunlight and shadows. The laboratory model learns to associate specific lab background artifacts with classes rather than true disease morphology.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> How does Data Augmentation (random rotations, horizontal flips, color jitter, CutMix) help bridge the gap between lab datasets and real-world farm conditions?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Data augmentation simulates physical outdoor variability during training. Random brightness/contrast jittering mimics shifting sunlight and passing clouds; random zooming and rotations mimic how a farmer holds a smartphone at various angles and distances; CutMix forces the model to look at multiple leaf regions rather than memorizing a single studio corner.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Data Augmentation Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration, what visual transformation pipeline simulated the variable outdoor smartphone captures of rural smallholders?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Random horizontal/vertical flips, affine rotations (±30°), color jitter (brightness, contrast, saturation), and perspective warping</li>\n                        <li>B) Inverting all pixel colors to negatives</li>\n                        <li>C) Blurring all images to a solid gray screen</li>\n                        <li>D) Renaming the image files</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Random flips, rotations, color jitter, and perspective warping simulated real outdoor camera variability.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Comprehensive data augmentation introduces realistic stochastic perturbations, preventing convolutional networks from overfitting to static lab orientations and studio lighting.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Negative color inversion distorts natural chlorophyll pigments and produces unrealistic samples.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Blurring to solid gray annihilates all spatial lesion signal.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Renaming files does not alter image tensor pixel values.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Domain Shift Stress-Test Studio):</strong> During the Domain Shift Studio, what diagnostic test proved that a model trained on lab leaves had memorized the gray cardboard background rather than the disease lesion?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Saliency map / Grad-CAM visualization showing that the neural network\'s activation heat focused on the cardboard background corners rather than the infected leaf tissue</li>\n                        <li>B) The computer monitor turned gray</li>\n                        <li>C) The file format changed from PNG to GIF</li>\n                        <li>D) Model loss became exactly zero</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Grad-CAM heatmaps highlighted background corners rather than actual biological disease lesions.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Explainable AI tools (Grad-CAM) compute gradients of target classes relative to feature maps, visually exposing \'Clever Hans\' effects where models rely on spurious background artifacts.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Grad-CAM visualizes network attention; it does not change monitor hardware.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Model explainability does not alter image file formats on disk.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Background memorization can still produce low training loss, but fails validation.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building an Agricultural Data Augmentation Pipeline, which modern Keras/PyTorch layer applied random zoom and rotation transformations dynamically on the GPU during training?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) tf.keras.layers.RandomRotation(0.2) and RandomZoom(0.2) (or torchvision.transforms.v2)</li>\n                        <li>B) tf.keras.layers.Dense(10)</li>\n                        <li>C) tf.print()</li>\n                        <li>D) np.save()</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Dynamic augmentation layers applied random rotations and zooms on GPU tensors during training batches.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Modern preprocessing layers execute directly on GPU memory pipelines during training, generating infinite stochastic variations without expanding physical disk storage.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Dense layers compute fully-connected linear matrix multiplications, not geometric image augmentations.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> tf.print is a tensor logging statement.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> np.save serializes arrays to disk.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm3-t12': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why is Object Detection (YOLO) required for real-time robotic weed spraying rather than standard Image Classification?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Image classification answers *what* is in the image (e.g., \'Weed present: Yes\'), but provides zero spatial coordinates. A precision robotic sprayer moving at 15 km/h needs to know *where* the weed is located down to the exact millimeter to trigger an ultra-targeted micro-nozzle burst. YOLO outputs precise bounding box coordinates `[x_center, y_center, width, height]` and class labels in a single 10-millisecond inference pass.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> How does Non-Maximum Suppression (NMS) prevent a robotic sprayer from spraying the exact same weed 20 times?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> YOLO predicts multiple overlapping candidate bounding boxes for a single object. Non-Maximum Suppression (NMS) sorts candidate boxes by confidence score, selects the highest-scoring box, and discards all other overlapping candidate boxes that share an Intersection-over-Union (IoU) greater than a set threshold (e.g., IoU > 0.45). This guarantees exactly one unified target box per weed.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live YOLO Inference Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration of real-time YOLOv8 pest detection on video, what output format was returned for each detected insect in the camera frame?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Bounding box coordinates [x, y, w, h], a class identifier string, and a detection confidence score between 0.0 and 1.0</li>\n                        <li>B) A single binary boolean True/False</li>\n                        <li>C) A 100-page PDF report</li>\n                        <li>D) An audio alert tone only</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Bounding box coordinates [x, y, w, h], class ID, and confidence score.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Single-stage object detectors (YOLO) formulate detection as spatial regression and classification, returning bounding box coordinates alongside class IDs and confidence probabilities in a single forward pass.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Binary booleans indicate presence/absence (classification), but provide no spatial coordinate localization.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Real-time edge detectors process 30 frames per second and do not generate static PDF files.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Raw inference outputs numeric tensors, which downstream apps can map to alerts.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Precision Robotic Sprayer Studio):</strong> During the Robotic Sprayer Architecture Studio, how did teams prevent redundant spray bursts when multiple candidate bounding boxes overlapped the same weed?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Applying Non-Maximum Suppression (NMS) with an Intersection-over-Union (IoU) threshold of 0.45 to eliminate duplicate candidate boxes</li>\n                        <li>B) Turning off the sprayer nozzles permanently</li>\n                        <li>C) Averaging the colors of all weeds</li>\n                        <li>D) Decreasing tractor speed to zero</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Non-Maximum Suppression (NMS) with IoU thresholding pruned redundant candidate bounding boxes.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> NMS eliminates redundant overlapping candidate boxes by measuring spatial overlap (IoU) against the highest-confidence anchor, ensuring each individual weed receives a single actuation pulse.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Turning off nozzles stops all weed control and destroys crop yield.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Averaging colors does not resolve overlapping bounding box coordinates.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Halting tractor movement stops all commercial field operations.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task running YOLOv8 pest detection, what parameter filtered out low-confidence background false alarms?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) model.predict(source=\'crop.jpg\', conf=0.50)</li>\n                        <li>B) model.predict(source=\'crop.jpg\', speed=100)</li>\n                        <li>C) model.delete()</li>\n                        <li>D) model.export(format=\'txt\')</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>The conf=0.50 parameter discarded all candidate bounding boxes with confidence below 50%.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Setting the `conf` confidence threshold filters out weak candidate predictions, suppressing background false positives while retaining high-certainty pest and weed detections.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> speed is not a valid inference parameter in Ultralytics YOLO.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> delete() deletes the model object from memory.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> exporting to text converts model weights without performing inference.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm3-t13': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why is classifying soil texture (Sand, Silt, Clay) based on the USDA Soil Textural Triangle the fundamental first step in precision agriculture?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Soil texture dictates hydrological and chemical behavior! Sandy soils drain rapidly, suffer nutrient leaching, and require frequent micro-irrigation. Clay soils hold vast water volumes but risk waterlogging and compaction. A precision fertilizer and irrigation algorithm will fail if it applies the same water schedule to sandy loam as it does to heavy clay.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> What is a multi-parameter Soil Health Index (SHI), and why is chemical fertility (N-P-K) only one-third of the health equation?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> True soil health has three interconnected pillars: Chemical (pH, organic carbon, N-P-K, CEC), Physical (aggregate stability, bulk density, water infiltration rate), and Biological (microbial biomass carbon, earthworm activity, respiration). High chemical N-P-K is useless if the soil is severely compacted (poor physical health) with dead microbial ecology (poor biological health).</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Soil Triangle Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration of soil texture classification, what mathematical constraint applies to the percentages of Sand, Silt, and Clay for any valid physical soil sample?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) % Sand + % Silt + % Clay must sum exactly to 100.0%</li>\n                        <li>B) Sand must always be greater than Clay</li>\n                        <li>C) The product of Sand and Silt must equal zero</li>\n                        <li>D) Each component must be an odd number</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Sand, Silt, and Clay are compositional fractions that must sum strictly to 100.0%.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Soil texture represents the physical particle size distribution of the mineral soil fraction; because it is compositional data, Sand + Silt + Clay must equal 100%, defining points within a ternary simplex triangle.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Heavy clay soils have much higher clay fractions than sand.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Loamy soils contain balanced amounts of both sand and silt, so their product is positive.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Fractions are continuous real numbers, not restricted to odd integers.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Soil Health Index (SHI) Studio):</strong> During the Soil Health Index Studio, what non-linear scoring function transformed raw soil pH into a standardized [0, 1] biological health score?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) A Gaussian bell-shaped scoring curve peaking at optimal neutral pH (6.5–7.0) and penalizing extreme acidity (<5.0) and alkalinity (>8.5)</li>\n                        <li>B) A strictly linear upward line where higher pH is always better</li>\n                        <li>C) Setting all scores to 1.0 unconditionally</li>\n                        <li>D) Multiplying pH by -1</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>A Gaussian scoring function peaking at optimal neutral pH (6.5–7.0) and penalizing extremes.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Biological soil health metrics follow \'more is better\' (organic matter), \'less is better\' (salinity/heavy metals), or \'optimum is best\' (pH). Optimal nutrient availability occurs in neutral pH (6.5–7.0), modeled via Gaussian bell curves.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> High pH (>8.5) causes extreme sodicity and micronutrient lockup; linear upward scoring is agronomic nonsense.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Constant 1.0 scores eliminate diagnostic differentiation.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Negative pH scores are mathematically invalid in soil chemistry.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building a Soil Texture Classifier, which Scikit-Learn model classified continuous Sand-Silt-Clay ratios into discrete USDA textural classes (e.g., Sandy Loam, Silty Clay)?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) sklearn.ensemble.RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=100, max_depth=8)</li>\n                        <li>B) sklearn.linear_model.LinearRegression()</li>\n                        <li>C) scipy.spatial.distance.cosine()</li>\n                        <li>D) np.histogram()</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>RandomForestClassifier classified compositional soil particle fractions into USDA texture classes.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Random Forest is ideal for multi-class categorical classification on tabular compositional metrics, capturing non-linear boundary intersections on the USDA textural triangle.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> LinearRegression predicts continuous numbers, not categorical USDA textural classes.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Cosine distance is a distance metric, not a trained classifier estimator.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> np.histogram computes bin frequencies, not predictive classification.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm3-t14': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> How can shining a visible and near-infrared (Vis-NIR) light beam onto a dry soil sample predict its organic carbon, nitrogen, and clay content in 30 seconds without wet chemistry?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Molecular vibration spectroscopy! Organic carbon and soil minerals contain distinct chemical bonds (C-H, O-H, N-H, Al-OH) that absorb specific wavelengths of light in the Vis-NIR spectrum (350–2500 nm) due to molecular bending and stretching overtones. By recording the reflected spectral curve, machine learning algorithms map spectral absorption dips directly to laboratory soil nutrient concentrations.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> Why does a spectral soil model calibrated in Karnataka often fail when predicting soil nitrogen on farms in Uttar Pradesh?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Calibration Transfer / Domain Shift! Soil spectral signatures are influenced by underlying parent rock minerology, soil moisture, and iron oxide content (e.g., tropical red Alfisols vs northern Indo-Gangetic alluvial Inceptisols). Spectral regression models require local re-calibration or domain adaptation (e.g., Transfer Learning, Memory-Based Learning) to operate across distinct geological soil orders.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Vis-NIR Spectroscopy Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration of proximal soil spectroscopy, which spectral absorption band regions were strongly correlated with soil organic carbon (SOC)?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Vis-NIR overtones and combination bands in the shortwave infrared region (1400 nm, 1900 nm, and 2200 nm) corresponding to C-H, O-H, and N-H molecular bond vibrations</li>\n                        <li>B) FM radio broadcast frequencies (100 MHz)</li>\n                        <li>C) Cosmic gamma ray bands</li>\n                        <li>D) Pure ultraviolet radiation alone</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>SWIR overtones (1400, 1900, 2200 nm) corresponding to C-H, O-H, and N-H molecular bond absorptions.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Soil organic matter and clay minerals exhibit characteristic absorption features in the NIR and SWIR regions due to vibrational overtones of fundamental C-H and O-H stretching modes.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> FM radio waves have meter-scale wavelengths and do not resolve molecular soil chemical bonds.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Gamma rays are ionizing radiation not used in farm optical spectroscopy.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> UV radiation causes fluorescence but lacks the characteristic absorption overtones of organic carbon.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Calibration Transfer Studio):</strong> During the Proximal Sensor Fusion Studio, what mathematical preprocessing technique removed baseline spectral scattering drift caused by soil particle size roughness?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Standard Normal Variate (SNV) transformation combined with Savitzky-Golay first-derivative filtering</li>\n                        <li>B) Deleting all odd-numbered wavelengths</li>\n                        <li>C) Multiplying the reflectance spectrum by zero</li>\n                        <li>D) Sorting wavelength channels randomly</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Standard Normal Variate (SNV) and Savitzky-Golay derivation eliminated physical scatter drift.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Chemometric preprocessing using SNV centers and scales individual spectra to correct for physical particle scattering, while Savitzky-Golay differentiation removes baseline drift and accentuates subtle absorption peaks.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Deleting odd wavelengths cuts spectral resolution by half without removing physical scatter.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Multiplying by zero erases the entire spectral dataset.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Random sorting destroys continuous spectral absorption geometry.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task predicting soil nitrogen from sensor telemetry, what Scikit-Learn regression algorithm captured complex non-linear spectral interactions while providing feature importances?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) sklearn.ensemble.RandomForestRegressor(n_estimators=100, random_state=42)</li>\n                        <li>B) sklearn.naive_bayes.BernoulliNB()</li>\n                        <li>C) sklearn.cluster.DBSCAN()</li>\n                        <li>D) sklearn.decomposition.PCA() alone</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>RandomForestRegressor predicted continuous nitrogen ppm while resisting spectral collinearity.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> `RandomForestRegressor` handles high-dimensional, non-linear sensor arrays without severe collinearity breakdown, producing continuous nutrient concentration estimates with MDI/Permutation importance.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> BernoulliNB is a binary discrete classifier, incapable of predicting continuous nutrient concentrations.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> DBSCAN is an unsupervised density-based clustering tool.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> PCA performs dimensionality reduction, but requires a downstream regressor to output predictions.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm3-t15': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why is prescribing fertilizer based on Soil Test Crop Response (STCR) equations vastly superior to traditional blanket state-level fertilizer recommendations?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Blanket recommendations prescribe an identical generic dose (e.g., \'100 kg N/ha\') regardless of what is already present in the soil. If a farmer\'s plot already has 350 kg/ha available nitrogen from previous manure applications, blanket application wastes money and causes lodging. STCR equations calculate nutrient dosage scientifically: `Fertilizer Dose = Target Yield Requirement - Soil Nutrient Contribution - Organic Manure Contribution`, adjusting application dynamically to achieve a specific target yield.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> What is the \'Law of the Minimum\' (Liebig\'s Law), and why will a farmer waste money on expensive Nitrogen fertilizer if soil Zinc is deficient?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Plant growth is controlled not by total resources available, but by the *scarcest* resource (the limiting factor)! If soil zinc is deficient, crop yield will be capped at the zinc ceiling. Adding more nitrogen will not increase yield by even 1 kg; the extra nitrogen remains unabsorbed, leaching into waterways while wasting farmer capital. Precision nutrition requires balanced stoichiometric fertilization.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live STCR Equation Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration of Soil Test Crop Response (STCR) calibration for rice, what was the mathematical form of the fertilizer nitrogen prescription equation?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Fertilizer_N (kg/ha) = (Nutrient_Required_per_Quintal * Target_Yield) - (Soil_Efficiency_Factor * Available_Soil_N)</li>\n                        <li>B) Fertilizer_N = Target_Yield * 1000</li>\n                        <li>C) Fertilizer_N = Available_Soil_N / 2</li>\n                        <li>D) Fertilizer_N = 50 kg/ha for all farms</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Fertilizer_N = (Requirement * Target_Yield) - (Soil_Contribution) accounting for soil nutrient credits.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> STCR calibration models subtract the indigenous soil nutrient supply (calibrated by nutrient uptake efficiency) from the total nutrient requirement of the desired target yield, calculating the exact fertilizer deficit.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Multiplying target yield by 1000 produces astronomical, crop-lethal chemical dosages.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Dividing soil N by 2 ignores the target yield goal completely.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Constant 50 kg/ha is traditional blanket application, the antithesis of precision STCR.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Target Yield Prescription Studio):</strong> During the Target Yield Studio, why did student teams cap a farmer\'s target yield goal at 70 quintals/ha instead of allowing an unrealistic target of 150 quintals/ha for rainfed sorghum?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Because physiological genetic yield ceilings and regional agro-climatic water limits prevent rainfed sorghum from achieving 150 q/ha, and prescribing fertilizer for unattainable targets wastes inputs and burns crops</li>\n                        <li>B) Because the computer algorithm could not calculate numbers over 100</li>\n                        <li>C) Because the state government limits farmer profits</li>\n                        <li>D) Because fertilizer bags only come in packs of 10</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Capped targets to biological genetic and hydrological limits to prevent toxic over-fertilization.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Target yield models must respect biological and climatic ceilings (water availability, solar radiation, crop genetics). Setting unattainable yield goals results in massive, toxic chemical over-recommendations.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Modern computers calculate arbitrary precision floating-point numbers without integer caps at 100.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Government policies do not restrict biological harvest yields.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Package sizes have zero bearing on agronomic crop physiology limits.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task implementing an STCR Fertilizer Calculator, how did you verify that the function returned zero fertilizer if the soil was already super-saturated with nutrients?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Using max(0, calculated_fertilizer_dose) to prevent negative chemical application prescriptions</li>\n                        <li>B) Allowing negative fertilizer numbers to be printed</li>\n                        <li>C) Multiplying negative numbers by -1 to prescribe positive fertilizer</li>\n                        <li>D) Raising a fatal system crash</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Applied max(0, calculated_dose) ensuring nutrient-rich soils received zero fertilizer recommendations.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> If indigenous soil nutrient reserves exceed the target crop demand, the STCR formula yields a negative number. Real-world agronomic controllers clamp the output at zero (`max(0, dose)`), advising zero application.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Prescribing negative fertilizer confuses farmers and breaks automated spreader machinery.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Inverting negative signs prescribes positive fertilizer to already over-fertilized land.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Fertile soil is a positive agricultural condition and should not crash software.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm3-t16': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> A farmer needs to supply 120 kg N, 60 kg P2O5, and 40 kg K2O per hectare. Why is choosing between Urea, DAP, MOP, and complex 20:20:0:13 a complex mathematical optimization problem rather than a simple shopping trip?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Commercial fertilizers are multi-nutrient packages! For example, DAP (Di-Ammonium Phosphate) contains 18% Nitrogen and 46% Phosphorus. If you buy DAP to satisfy phosphorus, you automatically add nitrogen. Determining the exact combination of commercial fertilizer bags that meets all N-P-K constraints at the absolute lowest cost in rupees requires Linear Programming (Simplex method).</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> What happens if a fertilizer linear program has contradictory constraints (e.g., minimum nutrient requirements cannot be met without exceeding maximum toxicity bounds)?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Infeasible Solution! The linear programming solver (like `scipy.optimize.linprog`) returns `success: False` with status \'infeasible\'. This alerts the agronomist that no commercial blend of the selected fertilizers can satisfy the requirements, requiring adding supplementary single-nutrient fertilizers (like straight Urea or MOP) or relaxing constraints.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Linear Programming Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration of cost-minimized fertilizer blending using scipy.optimize.linprog, what represented the objective function to be minimized?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Total procurement cost: minimize c^T * x, where c is the vector of bag prices in rupees and x is the number of bags of each fertilizer type</li>\n                        <li>B) The weight of the tractor trailer</li>\n                        <li>C) The chemical formula molecular weight of Urea</li>\n                        <li>D) Total rainfall in the district</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Total procurement cost: minimize c^T * x subject to linear N-P-K inequality constraints.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> In commercial farm economics, fertilizer blending is formulated as a classic linear program: minimizing total purchase expenditure subject to meeting minimum required N, P, and K crop uptake constraints.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Trailer weight is a logistics transport factor, not the chemical nutrient optimization objective.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Molecular weight is a physical chemistry constant, not an economic cost function.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Rainfall is an environmental exogenous variable.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Fertilizer Budget Optimization Studio):</strong> During the Cooperative Budget Studio, what linear inequality constraints ensured that all minimum nutrient requirements were met across 1,000 hectares?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) A_ub @ x <= -b_ub (negating inequality coefficients to enforce nutrient_delivered >= minimum_nutrient_required)</li>\n                        <li>B) x == 0</li>\n                        <li>C) sum(x) > 1,000,000</li>\n                        <li>D) A_ub @ x == 0</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Negated inequality constraints (A_ub @ x <= -b) to enforce greater-than-or-equal nutrient targets.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Standard linear programming solvers solve minimization subject to `<=`. To enforce minimum lower bounds (`A @ x >= b`), standard formulation multiplies both sides by -1: `-A @ x <= -b`.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Setting x == 0 purchases zero fertilizer, starving all crops.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Arbitrary bag sum constraints do not guarantee balanced N-P-K stoichiometry.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Enforcing zero nutrient delivery leads to complete crop failure.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task solving fertilizer cost minimization with SciPy, which method call returned the optimal bag quantities and confirmed mathematical convergence?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) res = scipy.optimize.linprog(c, A_ub=A_ub, b_ub=b_ub, bounds=bounds, method=\'highs\') with res.success == True</li>\n                        <li>B) scipy.fft()</li>\n                        <li>C) np.linalg.det()</li>\n                        <li>D) res = \'Optimal\'</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>linprog(..., method=\'highs\') verified successful convergence and extracted optimal bag quantities.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> SciPy\'s modern `highs` solver implements high-performance dual simplex and interior-point algorithms, returning optimal decision variable solutions and convergence verification via `res.success`.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> fft() is for frequency signal processing, not linear optimization.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> det() computes matrix determinants.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Assigning a hardcoded string does not execute numerical mathematical programming.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm3-t17': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why is irrigating crops based on fixed clock timers (e.g., \'turn pump on every morning at 6:00 AM for 30 minutes\') an agronomic and ecological disaster?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Clock timers ignore environmental demand! On a cloudy, cool day following a rainstorm, the soil is already saturated; the timer waters anyway, causing root rot, fungus, electricity waste, and nutrient leaching. On a hot, windy afternoon with high evapotranspiration, the timer provides insufficient water, causing severe plant drought stress. Precision irrigation uses closed-loop IoT soil tension thresholds and weather-aware controllers.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> What is \'Soil Water Tension\' (measured in centibars/kPa by tensiometers), and why does it represent plant thirst far better than volumetric water percentage (VWC %)?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> VWC % tells you how much water is present, but not how tightly the soil holds it! A clay soil at 25% moisture holds water so tightly in tiny micro-pores that plant roots cannot extract it (plants wilt). A sandy soil at 15% moisture releases water freely. Soil water tension measures the physical suction energy required by roots to extract water, providing a universal measure of plant water availability across all soil types.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Closed-Loop Irrigation Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration of closed-loop irrigation actuation, what hysteresis mechanism prevented the solenoid valve from rapidly chattering on and off when moisture hovered around the threshold?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) A dual-threshold deadband: opening the valve when moisture drops below 22% (start), but closing it only after moisture reaches 32% (field capacity)</li>\n                        <li>B) Disconnecting the solenoid valve wires</li>\n                        <li>C) Turning on the pump permanently</li>\n                        <li>D) Rounding all sensor numbers to 100</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Dual-threshold deadband (hysteresis) prevented valve chattering by separating start and stop thresholds.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Hysteresis prevents actuator wear and hydraulic pipe hammer: separating the activation threshold (22% deficit) from the deactivation threshold (32% field capacity) ensures long, stable irrigation cycles.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Disconnecting solenoid wires stops all automated irrigation capability.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Continuous pumping floods fields and burns pump motors.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Rounding to 100 causes the system to believe soil is perpetually soaked, preventing any watering.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Solar Precision Drip Architecture Studio):</strong> During the Solar Precision Irrigation Studio, how did teams synchronize variable-frequency drive (VFD) solar pump speeds with crop water demand?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) By mapping solar PV irradiance curves to pulse-width modulated (PWM) drip valve zones, prioritizing peak afternoon pumping during maximum solar generation</li>\n                        <li>B) By running the solar pump strictly at midnight</li>\n                        <li>C) By connecting solar panels directly to ground soil</li>\n                        <li>D) By draining the borehole completely every day</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Synchronized VFD solar pump output with diurnal solar generation and peak afternoon crop transpiration.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Solar drip systems match generation with demand: peak solar energy generation coincides with peak afternoon vapor pressure deficit, allowing VFD pumps to deliver maximum water during high evapotranspiration periods.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Solar panels produce zero energy at midnight without expensive grid-scale batteries.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Connecting panels to soil shorts the electrical system and risks electrocution.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Depleting boreholes destroys rural groundwater aquifers.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building a Crop-Stage Aware Irrigation Controller, how did the decision boundary adjust irrigation thresholds when maize transitioned from vegetative growth to flowering (tasseling)?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) It raised the moisture trigger threshold from 20% to 30%, prioritizing high moisture during the drought-sensitive flowering pollination stage</li>\n                        <li>B) It shut off irrigation completely during flowering</li>\n                        <li>C) It doubled the chemical pesticide rate instead of watering</li>\n                        <li>D) It deleted the soil sensor records</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Raised soil moisture triggers from 20% to 30% to protect critical reproductive pollination from drought.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Maize is hypersensitive to moisture stress during flowering/silking: a 2-day water deficit during pollination can reduce final grain yield by 50%. The stage-aware controller tightens moisture bounds during critical phenology.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Cutting water during flowering causes complete pollination failure and ear abortion.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Pesticides cannot substitute for physical plant hydration.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Deleting records prevents all automated control.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm3-t18': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why does the internationally accepted FAO-56 Penman-Monteith method calculate Reference Evapotranspiration (ET0) from a hypothetical grass surface rather than measuring actual crop transpiration directly?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Standardization! Actual crop water use depends on thousands of variable crop cultivars, planting densities, and stages. FAO-56 standardizes atmospheric evaporative demand into a single universal reference: a hypothetical, well-watered 0.12m grass surface (ET0). Once atmospheric demand (ET0) is known from weather data, actual crop water use is calculated via simple crop coefficients: `ETc = Kc * ET0`.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> How does the crop coefficient (Kc) change over the lifecycle of a wheat crop from germination to maturity, and how does this dictate irrigation volume?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Dynamic biological curve! At initial emergence (germination), Kc is low (~0.3–0.4) because the canopy is tiny and water loss is mostly soil evaporation. During mid-season peak vegetative growth and flowering, Kc spikes to 1.15–1.20 (transpiring 20% *more* water than reference grass). At late-season ripening, leaves senesce and Kc drops to 0.25–0.40 as the crop dries for harvest.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live FAO-56 Penman-Monteith Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration calculating Reference Evapotranspiration (ET0), which 4 meteorological variables were required to compute the Penman-Monteith energy balance equation?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Net solar radiation, ambient air temperature, relative humidity (or vapor pressure), and wind speed measured at 2m height</li>\n                        <li>B) Soil nitrogen, phosphorus, potassium, and soil pH</li>\n                        <li>C) Tractor diesel consumption, oil pressure, battery voltage, and tire wear</li>\n                        <li>D) Latitude, longitude, and elevation alone without any weather sensors</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Net solar radiation, air temperature, relative humidity, and 2m wind speed.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> The Penman-Monteith equation combines radiation energy balance (driving liquid-to-vapor phase change) with aerodynamic vapor transport (wind speed and vapor pressure deficit removing saturated air from the boundary layer).</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Soil chemical nutrients govern plant fertility, but do not dictate atmospheric physical evapotranspiration.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Machinery telemetry is completely irrelevant to atmospheric physics.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Geographic coordinates alone cannot measure instantaneous daily atmospheric weather conditions.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Basin-Scale Water Budgeting Studio):</strong> During the Hydrological Water Budgeting Studio, how did teams calculate daily crop evapotranspiration (ETc) for a 50-hectare potato field during peak tuber bulking stage?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) ETc = Kc * ET0, multiplying reference ET0 by the potato crop coefficient Kc = 1.15</li>\n                        <li>B) ETc = ET0 / Kc</li>\n                        <li>C) ETc = ET0 + 100 mm</li>\n                        <li>D) ETc = 0 mm</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>ETc = Kc * ET0, scaling atmospheric demand by the specific crop phenology coefficient.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> The standard FAO two-step methodology multiplies atmospheric evaporative demand (ET0) by the crop-specific growth stage coefficient (Kc) to determine exact daily crop water consumption.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Dividing ET0 by Kc reverses the biological scaling relationship.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Adding 100mm would drown the potato crop with extreme flooding.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Living crops transpire continuously during sunny days; ETc cannot be zero.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task calculating Daily Crop Evapotranspiration, what conversion factor converted ETc (in millimeters of water depth) into total liters of water required for a 2.5-hectare plot?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) 1 mm of water over 1 hectare = 10,000 liters, so Total Liters = ETc_mm * 2.5 * 10,000</li>\n                        <li>B) Total Liters = ETc_mm * 2.5</li>\n                        <li>C) Total Liters = ETc_mm / 1000</li>\n                        <li>D) Total Liters = ETc_mm ** 2</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Multiplied ETc_mm * hectares * 10,000 L/ha-mm to compute absolute volumetric water requirement in liters.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Hydraulic volumetric conversion: 1 mm depth across 1 square meter = 1 liter. Since 1 hectare = 10,000 m², 1 mm across 1 ha equals exactly 10,000 liters of water.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Failing to apply the 10,000 area scalar leaves volume underestimated by a factor of 10,000.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Dividing by 1000 reduces volume to near zero.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Squaring millimeters violates volumetric dimensional units.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm3-t19': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why is an agricultural commodity price time series (e.g., onion prices at Lasalgaon Mandi) non-stationary, and why does non-stationarity destroy standard statistical forecasting models?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Non-stationarity means statistical properties (mean, variance, autocorrelation) change over time. Mandi prices exhibit strong seasonal harvest cycles, multi-year inflation trends, and extreme monsoon volatility. Classical statistical models require stationarity—constant mean and variance over time. Modeling non-stationary prices directly leads to spurious correlations and explosive forecast intervals. Stationarity is achieved through differencing (`diff()`) or log-transformation.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> How does \'Additive\' versus \'Multiplicative\' time series decomposition apply to seasonal agricultural rainfall and crop yield?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> In Additive decomposition `Y(t) = Trend + Seasonal + Residual`, the seasonal swings have constant amplitude regardless of the trend level. In Multiplicative decomposition `Y(t) = Trend * Seasonal * Residual`, seasonal swings expand proportionally as the trend grows (e.g., seasonal price fluctuations grow wider as overall crop value increases). Most agricultural economics follows multiplicative dynamics.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Time Series Decomposition Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration of seasonal decomposition on 10 years of onion mandi prices, what did the Augmented Dickey-Fuller (ADF) test indicate prior to differencing?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) A p-value > 0.05, failing to reject the null hypothesis and confirming the price series was non-stationary with a unit root</li>\n                        <li>B) A p-value of 0.000001 proving perfect stationarity</li>\n                        <li>C) That onion prices never change</li>\n                        <li>D) That the database had zero records</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>p-value > 0.05 confirmed non-stationarity with a unit root, requiring differencing prior to modeling.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> In the ADF test, the null hypothesis states that a unit root is present (series is non-stationary). A p-value above 0.05 fails to reject the null, indicating trends or seasonality that require differencing.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Raw commodity price series with inflation trends and seasonal peaks are virtually never stationary without differencing.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Onion markets are famous for high price volatility.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> ADF tests evaluate existing numeric series, not empty databases.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Differencing & Stationarity Studio):</strong> During the Stationarity Studio, how did student teams transform a non-stationary agricultural price series with an upward trend and expanding seasonal variance into a stationary series?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Applying a logarithmic transformation to stabilize variance, followed by first-order seasonal differencing (diff(12)) to remove trend and seasonality</li>\n                        <li>B) Deleting all records from odd-numbered years</li>\n                        <li>C) Multiplying all prices by 100</li>\n                        <li>D) Replacing all prices with the average of the first month</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Log-transformation stabilized variance, and seasonal differencing (diff(12)) removed trend and seasonality.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> The classic Box-Jenkins methodology: log transformation converts multiplicative variance into additive variance, and subsequent differencing eliminates polynomial trends and annual seasonal cycles.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Deleting odd years destroys continuous chronological sequence and seasonality.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Multiplying by a constant scalar preserves non-stationarity perfectly.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Replacing data with a constant erases all historical price dynamics.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task performing Agro-Climatic Decomposition, which statsmodels function decomposed temperature time series into Trend, Seasonal, and Residual components?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) statsmodels.tsa.seasonal.seasonal_decompose(series, model=\'additive\', period=12)</li>\n                        <li>B) statsmodels.cluster()</li>\n                        <li>C) statsmodels.ols()</li>\n                        <li>D) statsmodels.filter_zeros()</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>seasonal_decompose() extracted underlying trend, repeating seasonal cycles, and irregular residuals.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> `seasonal_decompose` applies moving averages and seasonal averaging to separate a time series into distinct additive or multiplicative Trend, Seasonal, and Residual component arrays.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> statsmodels.cluster is a non-existent method.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> ols() fits ordinary least squares regression, not seasonal time-series decomposition.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> filter_zeros is not a statsmodels decomposition function.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm3-t20': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> What do the three parameters (p, d, q) in an ARIMA(p, d, q) model signify when forecasting daily agricultural temperatures?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> `p` is the Autoregressive (AR) order: predicting today\'s temperature using the linear combination of the previous `p` days\' temperatures. `d` is the Integrated (I) differencing order: how many times the series was differenced to achieve stationarity. `q` is the Moving Average (MA) order: modeling today\'s value based on the residual forecast errors of the previous `q` days.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> Why does pure ARIMA struggle when forecasting rainfall 30 days into the future compared to temperature?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Rainfall is zero-inflated and highly stochastic (mostly zero on dry days, followed by unpredictable extreme spike events during cloudbursts). Temperature is continuous, smooth, and governed by thermodynamic heat capacity. ARIMA excels on continuous Gaussian-like processes; for intermittent rainfall, models must combine Markov chain transition models (rain/no-rain) with Gamma distribution intensity models.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live ARIMA Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration fitting an ARIMA model to minimum daily temperatures for early frost warning, how were optimal (p, d, q) orders identified?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Inspecting Autocorrelation (ACF) and Partial Autocorrelation (PACF) plots combined with minimizing Akaike Information Criterion (AIC)</li>\n                        <li>B) Selecting random numbers between 1 and 100</li>\n                        <li>C) Setting p, d, and q all to zero</li>\n                        <li>D) Asking farmers to vote on parameters</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Analyzed ACF/PACF plots and selected model orders that minimized Akaike Information Criterion (AIC).</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> ARIMA model identification analyzes PACF cuts for AR order (p), ACF cuts for MA order (q), and compares AIC scores across candidate configurations to penalize overfitting while maximizing likelihood.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Random parameter guesses lead to divergent, uncalibrated forecasts.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> ARIMA(0,0,0) is white noise with zero predictive capability.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Statistical orders are mathematical properties of data, not subjective community votes.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Early Frost Warning Studio):</strong> During the Early Frost Warning Studio, why did teams focus on the lower bound of the ARIMA 95% confidence prediction interval rather than just the point forecast?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Because in frost risk management, if the lower 95% prediction interval drops below 0°C, emergency heaters and sprinklers must be staged even if the mean forecast is 2°C</li>\n                        <li>B) Because the lower interval is always 100% accurate</li>\n                        <li>C) Because temperatures cannot rise above the mean</li>\n                        <li>D) To save printer ink</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Used the lower 95% interval bound to trigger defensive frost mitigation against probabilistic downside freeze risk.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Agricultural frost protection is risk-averse: a single freeze kills an orchard. Operating on the lower 95% confidence bound accounts for atmospheric forecast uncertainty, alerting growers before temperatures dip below freezing.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> A 95% interval implies a 5% tail risk; it is not 100% deterministic.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Temperatures fluctuate symmetrically around point forecasts.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Digital monitoring dashboards do not consume printer ink.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task fitting an ARIMA model on temperature, which pmdarima function automated the iterative grid search for optimal seasonal orders (SARIMAX)?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) pmdarima.auto_arima(series, seasonal=True, m=12, stepwise=True, suppress_warnings=True)</li>\n                        <li>B) pmdarima.predict_all()</li>\n                        <li>C) pmdarima.clean_weather()</li>\n                        <li>D) pmdarima.delete_series()</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>pmdarima.auto_arima() performed automated stepwise AIC minimization to select optimal SARIMAX orders.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> `auto_arima` executes the automated Hyndman-Khandakar algorithm, conducting unit root tests, differencing, and stepwise search across AR, MA, and seasonal components to identify the model with lowest AIC.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> predict_all is a fictitious method.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> auto_arima fits statistical models; it does not clean raw weather datasets.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> delete_series is an invalid function.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm3-t21': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> How does Principal Component Analysis (PCA) combined with K-Means clustering allow meteorologists to discover new agro-climatic zones across thousands of weather stations?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Dimensionality reduction and pattern discovery! 50 weather stations each record 12 monthly temperatures, 12 monthly rainfalls, humidity, and solar radiation (hundreds of variables). PCA compresses these correlated multi-month metrics into 2 or 3 principal climate components (e.g., PC1 = Annual Heat Load, PC2 = Monsoon Intensity). K-Means then clusters stations into distinct agro-climatic zones based on natural climate topography without administrative district borders.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> Why is identifying \'Climate Shift Zone Migration\' critical for long-term farm planning and orchard investments?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Perennial fruit orchards (mango, apple, coffee) take 5–10 years to reach full bearing and live for 30–50 years. Due to climate change, historic weather clusters are shifting (e.g., apple chilling-hour zones migrating up mountains, or semi-arid zones expanding). Recognizing cluster migration allows farmers to plant climate-resilient rootstocks adapted to the climate of 2040 rather than 1990.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Climate Clustering Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration clustering 100 weather stations across Karnataka, what technique determined the optimal number of climate clusters (k)?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) The Elbow Method (inertia inflection point) combined with Silhouette Analysis to evaluate cluster separation</li>\n                        <li>B) Picking k = 100 so every station has its own cluster</li>\n                        <li>C) Choosing k equal to the number of political parties</li>\n                        <li>D) Setting k = 0</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Used the Elbow Method on within-cluster inertia alongside Silhouette Scores to locate optimal k.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Selecting optimal clustering k balances compactness (minimizing inertia on the elbow curve) with separation (maximizing silhouette coefficients), ensuring natural agro-climatic grouping without over-fragmentation.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> k = N trivializes clustering, providing zero generalization or data compression.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Political dynamics have zero scientific connection to meteorology.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> k must be an integer >= 1; k = 0 is mathematically invalid in K-Means.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Drought Vulnerability Clustering Studio):</strong> During the Vulnerability Clustering Studio, what unsupervised cluster was flagged as having the highest agricultural drought risk?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) The cluster exhibiting low total annual precipitation, high coefficient of variation in monsoon onset, and high average vapor pressure deficit (VPD)</li>\n                        <li>B) The cluster located in tropical coastal rainforests</li>\n                        <li>C) The cluster with zero days of sunlight</li>\n                        <li>D) The cluster with continuous snow cover</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>The cluster with low precipitation, erratic monsoon onset variance, and high atmospheric vapor pressure deficit.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Drought vulnerability is multidimensional: low rainfall combined with high temporal volatility (unreliable onset) and high atmospheric evaporative suction (high VPD) creates acute agrarian risk.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Coastal rainforests have high humidity and heavy rainfall, minimizing drought vulnerability.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Continuous zero sunlight does not exist in agricultural farming zones.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Snow cover reflects alpine/polar climates, not tropical drought vulnerability.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task clustering weather stations, which Scikit-Learn pipeline standardized weather metrics, reduced dimensions with PCA, and clustered with K-Means?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Pipeline([(\'scaler\', StandardScaler()), (\'pca\', PCA(n_components=2)), (\'kmeans\', KMeans(n_clusters=4, random_state=42))])</li>\n                        <li>B) Pipeline([(\'kmeans\', KMeans())]) without scaling</li>\n                        <li>C) StandardScaler() alone</li>\n                        <li>D) PCA(n_components=0)</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Chained StandardScaler, PCA(n_components=2), and KMeans in a Scikit-Learn Pipeline.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Clustering spatial weather stations requires feature standardization (preventing rainfall in mm from dominating temperature in °C), PCA dimensionality reduction, and K-Means partitioning in an integrated pipeline.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Clustering unscaled features allows large-scale variables to distort Euclidean distances completely.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> StandardScaler standardizes features, but performs no dimensionality reduction or clustering.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> PCA requires at least 1 principal component.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm3-t22': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why do agricultural commodity prices at APMC Mandis exhibit the famous \'Cobweb Phenomenon\' (violent multi-year boom-and-bust price cycles)?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Lagged supply response! When tomato prices spike to Rs. 100/kg due to a shortage, every farmer rushes to plant tomatoes next season. Three months later, a massive supply glut hits the mandi simultaneously. Prices crash to Rs. 2/kg, and farmers dump tomatoes on roads. Discouraged farmers refuse to plant tomatoes next season, causing a supply shortage that triggers the next massive price spike. AI forecasting models help farmers anticipate gluts before planting.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> How do Bollinger Bands help a farmer cooperative decide whether to sell their harvested grain immediately or store it in a warehouse?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Bollinger Bands plot a 20-day moving average flanked by upper and lower bands set 2 standard deviations away. If the current mandi price touches or punches through the lower band, the market is temporarily oversold (harvest glut peak). If seasonal forecasts indicate prices will rebound toward the mean within 60 days and storage costs are low, the cooperative profits by holding grain in cold storage rather than panic selling.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Mandi Price Trend Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration of APMC Mandi price analysis, what technical indicator signaled that onion prices had entered an oversold harvest glut condition?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) The daily mandi modal price broke below the lower Bollinger Band (2 standard deviations below the 20-day moving average)</li>\n                        <li>B) The price became exactly zero rupees</li>\n                        <li>C) The mandi closed permanently</li>\n                        <li>D) All onions turned green</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Modal price dropped below the lower Bollinger Band, indicating an extreme statistical oversold condition.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Bollinger Bands measure volatility: prices falling below the lower band (Mean - 2*sigma) represent extreme downward statistical deviations typical of peak post-harvest harvest gluts, signaling potential price recovery.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Commodity prices reflect market value and do not hit zero in organized mandis.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Periodic harvest gluts do not cause permanent market closure.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Onion skin color is unrelated to econometric technical indicators.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Mandi Storage Strategy Studio):</strong> During the Mandi Storage Strategy Studio, how did teams determine the break-even decision between selling harvested maize immediately versus renting warehouse space for 90 days?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Net Profit = Predicted_Price_90d - Current_Price - (Monthly_Warehouse_Rent * 3) - Storage_Shrinkage_Loss - Working_Capital_Interest; store only if Net Profit > 0</li>\n                        <li>B) Storing indefinitely regardless of storage fees</li>\n                        <li>C) Selling immediately regardless of market price</li>\n                        <li>D) Guessing based on personal superstition</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Calculated net gain after warehouse rent, physical grain shrinkage loss, and capital financing interest.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Grain post-harvest economics requires accounting for carrying costs: physical warehouse rent, moisture shrinkage (weight loss), spoilage risk, and the opportunity cost/interest on holding inventory.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Indefinite storage incurs compounding rental costs that eventually exceed grain value.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Panic selling during harvest gluts locks in the lowest prices of the entire year.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Agronomic and financial management relies on rigorous quantitative accounting, not superstition.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building a Moving Average Price Predictor, which Pandas code calculated 20-day and 50-day Simple Moving Averages (SMA) to identify golden crossover trading signals?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) df[\'sma_20\'] = df[\'modal_price\'].rolling(20).mean(); df[\'sma_50\'] = df[\'modal_price\'].rolling(50).mean()</li>\n                        <li>B) df[\'sma_20\'] = df[\'modal_price\'] * 20</li>\n                        <li>C) df[\'modal_price\'].cumsum()</li>\n                        <li>D) df[\'modal_price\'].mean()</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Computed 20-day and 50-day rolling means using rolling().mean().</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Calculating rolling window means over 20-day (short term) and 50-day (medium term) spans creates technical moving averages; when SMA-20 crosses above SMA-50, it generates a bullish price momentum signal.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Multiplying price by 20 magnifies prices into astronomical numbers.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> cumsum computes progressive cumulative totals, not moving averages.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> mean() computes a single static scalar average across the entire multi-year dataset.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm3-t23': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why is consumer demand for perishable vegetables (e.g., coriander, spinach, tomatoes) in urban metros far harder to forecast than demand for durable grains (e.g., wheat, rice)?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Perishability and extreme demand inelasticity! Green leafy vegetables have a post-harvest shelf life of only 24–48 hours without cold chains. Durable grains can be stored in silos for years to buffer demand shocks. Vegetable demand spikes wildly on specific festival days (Diwali, Eid, weddings) and collapses during heatwaves when spoilage accelerates, requiring high-frequency predictive logistics.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> How do \'Calendar Regressors\' (day-of-week, festival indicators, payday cycles) improve food supply chain machine learning models?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Human food purchasing habits are strongly periodic: supermarket footfall surges on weekends, temple visits increase demand for flowers and coconuts on Tuesdays and Fridays, and monthly salary disbursements drive bulk grocery purchases during the first week of each month. Embedding explicit cyclical calendar indicators allows algorithms to anticipate predictable demand surges.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Demand Forecasting Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration forecasting urban tomato demand with LightGBM, what engineered calendar feature captured the massive surge in restaurant vegetable consumption?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) A boolean \'is_weekend\' indicator (Friday-Sunday) combined with proximity to major public holidays and festive calendar flags</li>\n                        <li>B) The name of the delivery truck driver</li>\n                        <li>C) The phase of the moon alone</li>\n                        <li>D) The color of the vegetable crates</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Engineered weekend indicators and holiday/festival proximity features captured consumer purchasing cycles.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Food demand is heavily influenced by human institutional schedules: weekend dining out and holiday feasts drive predictable commercial kitchen demand surges that calendar features capture cleanly.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Driver identity has zero causal influence on city-wide consumer vegetable consumption.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Lunar phases have minimal impact on modern urban grocery demand.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Crate color is an operational packaging detail with no consumer demand signal.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Supply Chain Waste Reduction Studio):</strong> During the Perishable Food Waste Studio, how did teams balance the penalty of over-ordering (spoilage waste) versus under-ordering (stockout lost sales)?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) By training models with an Asymmetric Pinball Loss (Quantile Regression) tuned to optimize the 75th percentile of demand, minimizing expensive restaurant stockouts</li>\n                        <li>B) By ordering zero vegetables every day</li>\n                        <li>C) By ordering 1,000,000 tons of vegetables every day regardless of demand</li>\n                        <li>D) By deleting inventory spreadsheets</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Used Quantile Regression (Pinball Loss) to optimize specific percentiles balancing spoilage cost vs stockout penalty.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Standard MSE treats over-prediction and under-prediction symmetrically. In perishable supply chains, stockouts lose customers while over-ordering causes biological rotting. Quantile regression balances these asymmetric business costs.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Ordering zero halts supply chain operations completely.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Ordering 1,000,000 tons guarantees massive rotting waste and catastrophic financial loss.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Deleting spreadsheets destroys inventory management.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task modeling seasonal demand peaks, which Scikit-Learn transformer converted cyclical calendar months (1 to 12) into continuous periodic signals?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Sine and Cosine trigonometric encoding: sin(2*pi*month/12) and cos(2*pi*month/12)</li>\n                        <li>B) Ordinal integer encoding (1, 2, 3... 12)</li>\n                        <li>C) One-hot encoding with 500 dummy columns</li>\n                        <li>D) Converting months into random characters</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Cyclical Sine-Cosine encoding preserved the circular temporal continuity between December (12) and January (1).</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Integer encoding treats month 12 (December) and month 1 (January) as maximally distant (distance 11), when biologically they are adjacent winter months. Sine-Cosine encoding maps months onto a circle, preserving continuity.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Integer encoding introduces a false linear distance gap between December and January.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> One-hot encoding creates redundant feature dimensions without preserving cyclical distance.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Random characters eliminate all temporal meaning.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm3-t24': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> What is \'Spatial Price Transmission\' between agricultural wholesale markets, and why does a price spike in Delhi\'s Azadpur Mandi cause tomato prices to surge 1,500 km away in Kolar, Karnataka 48 hours later?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Spatial arbitrage and market integration! Wholesale agri-commodities trade across a national network. When supply drops in North India, wholesale traders dispatch refrigerated trucks to southern production hubs (like Kolar) offering higher prices. This inter-state purchasing pulls local supply out of Kolar, transmitting the price surge along the logistics corridor within the 48-hour transport transit time.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> Why do smallholder farmers often capture only 20–30% of the retail price paid by urban consumers for vegetables, and how does AI disintermediation alter this equation?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> The traditional agricultural supply chain has 5–7 intermediaries: village aggregators, commission agents, primary wholesale mandis, secondary terminal markets, semi-wholesalers, and street vendors. Each tier incurs transport costs, 10–15% post-harvest spoilage loss, and profit margins. AI-enabled direct Farm-to-Fork platforms use demand forecasting to bypass middlemen, sourcing directly from farmers to deliver 60%+ farmgate realization.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Spatial Arbitrage Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration of spatial price transmission between regional mandis, what mathematical condition determined whether inter-market arbitrage was economically viable?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Spatial Price Spread: Price_Destination - Price_Origin > Freight_Cost + Handling_Fee + In-Transit_Spoilage_Loss</li>\n                        <li>B) Destination price is lower than origin price</li>\n                        <li>C) The two mandis speak the same regional language</li>\n                        <li>D) Freight cost is infinite</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Arbitrage is profitable only when inter-mandi price spread exceeds total logistics, transit spoilage, and handling costs.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Spatial economic equilibrium dictates that commodities flow from surplus to deficit regions only when the price disparity exceeds the physical transaction friction (diesel freight, loading labor, and transit shrinkage/spoilage).</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Dispatching goods to a lower-priced market guarantees immediate trading losses.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Interstate trade flows operate across different linguistic states via common economic incentives.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Infinite freight cost prevents any physical transport.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Value Chain Disintermediation Studio):</strong> During the Value Chain Studio, what primary efficiency driver allowed direct digital B2B farm platforms to deliver higher prices to farmers while lowering costs for urban retailers?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Eliminating 4 intermediary handling tiers and utilizing demand forecasting to reduce perishable post-harvest spoilage from 25% down to under 5%</li>\n                        <li>B) Forcing farmers to work without payment</li>\n                        <li>C) Selling artificial synthetic plastic vegetables</li>\n                        <li>D) Evading all transport toll taxes</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Disintermediated 4 handling tiers and leveraged predictive demand to cut post-harvest waste from 25% to <5%.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Traditional physical supply chains destroy value through redundant physical loading/unloading and high spoilage. Direct digital pipelines connect farm harvesting directly to urban distribution centers based on algorithmic pre-orders.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Direct platforms pay farmers higher, guaranteed market-linked prices.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Platforms distribute fresh, certified agricultural produce.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Commercial logistics fleets pay all standard legal national highway tolls.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task analyzing Inter-Mandi Price Disparities, which econometric technique tested whether prices in Kolar and Delhi were cointegrated in long-run equilibrium?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Engle-Granger two-step cointegration test: checking if the residuals of regressing Price_Delhi on Price_Kolar are stationary</li>\n                        <li>B) Calculating the square root of the price sum</li>\n                        <li>C) Checking if both price numbers are even</li>\n                        <li>D) Plotting an empty graph</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Engle-Granger cointegration test verified that price residuals were stationary, proving long-run market integration.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Two non-stationary price series are cointegrated if a linear combination of them is stationary, proving that although daily prices wander, spatial arbitrage prevents them from drifting apart indefinitely.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Square roots of sums do not test cointegration relationships.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Even or odd integer values have zero econometric relevance.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Empty graphs display no diagnostic statistical information.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}}
